      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Solution: 1 has exactly 1 divisor (1), 15 has exactly 2 divisors (1 and 15), 14 has exactly 2 divisors (1 and 14), 3 has exactly 2 divisors (1 and 3), 2 has exactly 2 divisors (1 and 2), 6 has exactly 4 divisors (1, 2, 3, and 6), 12 has exactly 6 divisors (1, 2, 3, 4, 6, and 12), and 4 has exactly 2 divisors (1 and 4). Therefore, 6 has the greatest number of divisors. \\(_\\) \\({\\ }_\\) \\({\\ }_\\) \\({\\ }_\\) \n\nAnd the number that has the fewest divisors is \\boxed{15}.\n\nTo determine which number has the greatest number of divisors among the numbers 1, 2, 3, ..., 15, we evaluate the number of divisors for each of these numbers.\n\n- For 1: It is the smallest number, so it has only one divisor (the number itself). Therefore, 1 has only 1 divisor.\n- For 15: The divisors of 15 are 1, 3, 5, and 15. Thus, 15 has 4 divisors.\n- For 14: The divisors of 14 are 1, 2, 7, and 14. Therefore, 14 also has 4 divisors.\n- For 3: The divisors of 3 are 1 and 3. Hence, 3 has only 2 divisors.\n- For 2: The divisors of 2 are 1 and 2. Therefore, 2 has only 2 divisors.\n- For 6: The divisors of 6 are 1, 2, 3, and 6. So, 6 has only 4 divisors.\n- For 12: The divisors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, and 12. Therefore, 12 has 6 divisors.\n- For 4: The divisors of 4 are 1 and 4. Hence, 4 has only 2 divisors.\n\nFrom the above analysis, we see that 15 has the greatest number of divisors, which is 4. Thus, the number that has the greatest number of divisors is 15.\n\nThe answer $\\boxed{15}$ is obtained.",
